  "strategy": "### 1. Approach and Rationale  \n\nThe proposed approach combines convex relaxation with alternating direction method of multipliers (ADMM) for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. ADMM is ideal for handling the non-convex constraints and separable structure of the problem. The strategy uses a two-stage optimization:\n\n1. **Coarse Grid Optimization**: Start with the given initial_h_values (n_points=70) to quickly find a feasible solution.\n2. **Fine Grid Refinement**: Upsample to a higher resolution (n_points=280) for accurate modeling of h(x).\n\nThis approach balances exploration with exploitation, using ADMM to handle the constraints while allowing nonlinearities to escape convexity traps.\n\n### 2. Mathematical Specification  \n\n#### Variables  \n- \\( h \\in [0, 1]^{n_{points}} \\) : Step function samples  \n- \\( \\Delta x = 2 / n_{points} \\) : Grid spacing  \n\n#### Objective  \nMinimize:\n\\[\nC_5 = \\max_k \\left( \\vphantom{\\sum} \\text{np.correlate}(h, 1 - h, \\text{mode=\"full\"}) \\cdot \\Delta x \\right)\n\\]\n\n#### Constraints  \n1. \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) for all \\( i \\)\n2. \\( \\sum_{i=0}^{n_{points}-1} h[i] = n_{points} / 2 \\)\n\n#### ADMM Formulation  \nThe problem is reformulated as:\n\\[\n\\minimize_h \\quad \\max_k \\left( \\sum_{i=0}^{n_{points}-1} h_i (1 - h_{i + k}) \\Delta x \\right) \n\\]\nsubject to:\n\\[\nh_i \\in [0, 1], \\quad \\sum h_i = n_{points}/2\n\\]\nWe introduce auxiliary variables and dual multipliers to decompose the problem.\n\n### 3. Implementation Procedure  \n\n#### 1. Initialization  \n- Use initial_h_values if available. If not, initialize h with random values in [0, 1], normalized to meet the integral constraint.\n\n#### 2. Coarse Grid Optimization  \nFor n_points=70:\n1. Initialize ADMM variables:\n   - Primal variables \\( h, z \\)\n   - Dual multipliers \\( \\lambda \\)\n2. Iterate ADMM steps:\n   a. Update h by minimizing the augmented Lagrangian.\n   b. Update z to enforce \\( \\sum h = n_{points}/2 \\).\n   c. Update dual multipliers.\n3. After 200 iterations or convergence, upsample h to n_points=280.\n\n#### 3. Fine Grid Refinement  \nFor n_points=280:\n1. Initialize ADMM with upsampled h.\n2. Iterate ADMM steps with tighter stopping criteria.\n3. Project h to [0,1] and normalize after each iteration.\n\n#### 4. Validation and Termination  \n1. After each grid level, validate h against constraints.\n2. Track the best C\u2085 score and terminate if time budget exhausted or no improvement for 1000 iterations.\n\n### 4. Search Choices and Useful Variations  \n\n#### Default Configuration  \n- ADMM Parameters: \\( \\rho = 1.0 \\), \\( \\alpha = 0.9 \\)\n- Grid Levels: 70 \u2192 280 points\n- Max Iterations: 1000\n- Convergence Threshold: \\( 10^{-4} \\)\n\n#### Variations  \n1. **Parameter Adaptation**: Adjust \\( \\rho \\) dynamically based on residual norms.\n2. **Higher Resolutions**: Test n_points=560 for even finer control.\n\n### 5. Compute Budget and Robustness  \n\n#### Time Estimates  \n- Coarse grid: 200 iterations \u00d7 1ms = 0.2s\n- Fine grid: 1000 iterations \u00d7 1ms = 1s\n- Total: ~1.2s, well within 1100s budget.\n\n#### Robustness  \n- Regular constraint projection ensures feasibility.\n- Early stopping prevents overfitting to coarse grids.\n\n### 6. Validation and Return Contract  \n\n#### Feasibility Checks  \n- Ensure \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) via clipping.\n- Verify \\( \\sum h[i] = n_{points}/2 \\) via normalization.\n\n#### Final Evaluation  \n- Compute \\( C_5 \\) using the evaluator to confirm \u2264 0.380876.\n- Return the best valid h found.",
